{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This guide covers using Solid Query with server rendering, specifically with "},{"type":"link","href":"https://start.solidjs.com/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SolidStart"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you haven't already, it's worth reading the "},{"type":"link","href":"./prefetching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefetching & Router Integration"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide first."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"solid-query-s-hydration-model","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid Query's hydration model"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid Query's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" is built on top of Solid's own "},{"type":"link","href":"https://docs.solidjs.com/reference/basic-reactivity/create-resource","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createResource"}]},{"type":"text","value":", rather than a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dehydrate"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hydrate"},{"type":"text","value":" pair you call yourself. That has a consequence for this guide: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"there is no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dehydrate"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hydrate"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HydrationBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":" in your application code."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Instead, when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" runs on the server:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It resolves the query through the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient"},{"type":"text","value":" cache a client render would use, with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"retry"},{"type":"text","value":" forced off and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" forced on — see "},{"type":"link","href":"#error-handling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error handling"}]},{"type":"text","value":" below."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The resolved value is a plain, serializable snapshot of the query result (functions like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch"},{"type":"text","value":" are stripped, since they can't cross the network)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid's own resource streaming carries that snapshot down to the client — the same mechanism "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createResource"},{"type":"text","value":" uses for any async resource, query-related or not."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the client, once the snapshot arrives, Solid Query hydrates the query cache from it internally, before your component ever sees a loading state for that query."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So the three steps you'd normally implement by hand — prefetch, dehydrate, hydrate — collapse into \"call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" (or prefetch with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.query"},{"type":"text","value":"), and let Solid's streaming take care of the rest.\" The tradeoff is that Solid Query decides what gets serialized and when, rather than you calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dehydrate"},{"type":"text","value":" with your own filtering logic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One consequence worth calling out: a query that resolved on the server, and is read with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" (or without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":"), does not refetch on mount when it hydrates on the client, since Solid Query already knows it has fresh-enough data from the server render. It behaves the same as any other successful query after that: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" governs whether it refetches on the next trigger (window focus, a new mount, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invalidateQueries"},{"type":"text","value":", and so on)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"initial-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As with any server-rendered app, create the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient"},{"type":"text","value":" per request rather than at module scope, so cached data from one visitor's request is never shared with another's:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/app.tsx\nimport { MetaProvider, Title } from '@solidjs/meta'\nimport { Router } from '@solidjs/router'\nimport { FileRoutes } from '@solidjs/start/router'\nimport { Suspense } from 'solid-js'\nimport { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  // Constructing the QueryClient inside the component (rather than at module\n  // scope) means SolidStart creates a fresh one for every request, so data\n  // is never shared between users.\n  const queryClient = new QueryClient({\n    defaultOptions: {\n      queries: {\n        // staleTime governs refetches from later triggers (a new mount, window\n        // focus, and so on) — see Solid Query's hydration model above for why\n        // the very first mount after SSR doesn't need it to skip a refetch.\n        staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n      },\n    },\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n      <Router\n        root={(props) => (\n          <MetaProvider>\n            <Title>My App</Title>\n            <Suspense>{props.children}</Suspense>\n          </MetaProvider>\n        )}\n      >\n        <FileRoutes />\n      </Router>\n    </QueryClientProvider>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Suspense>"},{"type":"text","value":" boundary matters: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" suspends while a query is pending, both on the server (so SolidStart can wait for it, or stream past it) and on the client (during the very first render, before hydration data arrives)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-usequery-directly","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" directly"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because hydration is automatic, the simplest way to get server-rendered data is to just call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" where you need it — no separate prefetch step required:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\nimport { For, Suspense } from 'solid-js'\n\nfunction Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryFn: fetchPosts,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n      <ul>\n        <For each={query.data}>{(post) => <li>{post.title}</li>}</For>\n      </ul>\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the server, this fetches "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"posts"},{"type":"text","value":", waits for it (because of the surrounding "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Suspense>"},{"type":"text","value":"), and streams the resolved value down. On the client, that value hydrates the cache before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Posts"},{"type":"text","value":" ever renders a loading state — as long as the client also renders inside a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Suspense>"},{"type":"text","value":" boundary, which the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Suspense>"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"app.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" already provides."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wrap the same query in Solid's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ErrorBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":" to handle a failure. On the server, a failed query always throws to the nearest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ErrorBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":" — see "},{"type":"link","href":"#error-handling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error handling"}]},{"type":"text","value":" below for why, and for what it takes to get the same behavior on the client too:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\nimport { ErrorBoundary, For, Suspense } from 'solid-js'\n\nfunction Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryFn: fetchPosts,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <ErrorBoundary\n      fallback={(err, reset) => (\n        <div>\n          <div>Error: {err.message}</div>\n          <button\n            onClick={async () => {\n              await query.refetch()\n              reset()\n            }}\n          >\n            Retry\n          </button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    >\n      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n        <ul>\n          <For each={query.data}>{(post) => <li>{post.title}</li>}</For>\n        </ul>\n      </Suspense>\n    </ErrorBoundary>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prefetching-with-route-load","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefetching with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"route.load"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To prefetch a query from a SolidStart route's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"load"},{"type":"text","value":" function, call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.query(...)"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { noop, useQueryClient } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\n\nexport const route = {\n  load: () => {\n    const queryClient = useQueryClient()\n    queryClient.query(postsQueryOptions()).catch(noop)\n  },\n}\n\nexport default function Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => postsQueryOptions())\n  // ...\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":".catch(noop)"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps a failed prefetch from aborting routing — whatever reads the query afterwards ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":") will surface the failure on its own. See "},{"type":"link","href":"#error-handling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error handling"}]},{"type":"text","value":" below for how that failure surfaces differently on the server and the client."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sharing the query options object ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"postsQueryOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" above) between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"route.load"},{"type":"text","value":" and the component that reads the data, rather than repeating the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" in both places, keeps them from drifting apart — see "},{"type":"link","href":"./query-options.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"streaming-with-deferstream","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deferStream"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, a query streams to the client as soon as it resolves on the server. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deferStream: true"},{"type":"text","value":" to hold the server's response until that query resolves instead — useful for data that has to be present in the very first response, such as SEO meta tags:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const query = useQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['user'],\n  queryFn: fetchUser,\n  deferStream: true,\n}))","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deferStream"},{"type":"text","value":" only affects when the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server"}]},{"type":"text","value":" flushes that query's chunk to the client. Setting it on every query on a page makes the server wait for all of them before sending anything."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"error-handling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error handling"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the server, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" always throws when a query fails — regardless of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" option — so it's caught by the nearest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<ErrorBoundary>"},{"type":"text","value":" rather than surfacing as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"query.isError"},{"type":"text","value":". This is different from the client, where a failed query reports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isError"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" unless you opt in to throwing with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError: true"},{"type":"text","value":". Retries are also disabled on the server, so a query throws on its first failure rather than retrying first."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a query is read with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" on both the server and the client, set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError: true"},{"type":"text","value":" on it explicitly so the client matches what the server already does — otherwise the same failure throws during SSR but only sets "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isError"},{"type":"text","value":" once it's running on the client:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const query = useQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['user'],\n  queryFn: fetchUser,\n  throwOnError: true,\n}))","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"route.load"},{"type":"text","value":" prefetch isn't affected by this server-only override — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.query(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" doesn't take a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" option at all, so it simply rejects when the query fails, the same on the server and the client. Catch it (or let it propagate) however fits the route; see "},{"type":"link","href":"#prefetching-with-routeload","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefetching with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"route.load"}]},{"type":"text","value":" above for the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".catch(noop)"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern used when a failed prefetch shouldn't abort routing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tips-tricks-and-caveats","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tips, Tricks and Caveats"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"staleness-is-measured-from-when-the-query-was-fetched-on-the-server","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Staleness is measured from when the query was fetched on the server"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A query's staleness is based on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dataUpdatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":", which is set to when it resolved on the server, not when the client hydrates it. Because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":", an SSR'd query without an explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" is treated as stale as soon as its next trigger (window focus, a new mount past the first one, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invalidateQueries"},{"type":"text","value":", and so on) checks it, and may refetch in the background then — the first mount on the client is still exempt, per "},{"type":"link","href":"#solid-querys-hydration-model","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid Query's hydration model"}]},{"type":"text","value":" above. Setting a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" above "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" (as in the Initial setup example above) avoids this if you don't want it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"high-memory-consumption-on-server","children":[{"type":"text","value":"High memory consumption on server"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient"},{"type":"text","value":" is created fresh for every request, its cache is held in memory for the duration of that request. Under high request volume this can add up. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":" on the server, so unless you configure it lower, memory for a request's cache isn't reclaimed automatically — in most cases this is fine, since the whole "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient"},{"type":"text","value":" instance becomes eligible for garbage collection once the request finishes and nothing references it anymore."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"solid-query-s-hydration-model","text":"Solid Query's hydration model","level":2},{"id":"initial-setup","text":"Initial setup","level":2},{"id":"using-usequery-directly","text":"Using useQuery directly","level":2},{"id":"prefetching-with-route-load","text":"Prefetching with route.load","level":2},{"id":"streaming-with-deferstream","text":"Streaming with deferStream","level":2},{"id":"error-handling","text":"Error handling","level":2},{"id":"tips-tricks-and-caveats","text":"Tips, Tricks and Caveats","level":2},{"id":"staleness-is-measured-from-when-the-query-was-fetched-on-the-server","text":"Staleness is measured from when the query was fetched on the server","level":3},{"id":"high-memory-consumption-on-server","text":"High memory consumption on server","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThis guide covers using Solid Query with server rendering, specifically with [SolidStart](https://start.solidjs.com/).\n\nIf you haven't already, it's worth reading the [Prefetching & Router Integration](./prefetching.md) guide first.\n\n## Solid Query's hydration model\n\nSolid Query's `useQuery` is built on top of Solid's own [`createResource`](https://docs.solidjs.com/reference/basic-reactivity/create-resource), rather than a `dehydrate`/`hydrate` pair you call yourself. That has a consequence for this guide: **there is no `dehydrate`, `hydrate`, or `HydrationBoundary` in your application code.**\n\nInstead, when `useQuery` runs on the server:\n\n1. It resolves the query through the same `QueryClient` cache a client render would use, with `retry` forced off and `throwOnError` forced on — see [Error handling](#error-handling) below.\n2. The resolved value is a plain, serializable snapshot of the query result (functions like `refetch` are stripped, since they can't cross the network).\n3. Solid's own resource streaming carries that snapshot down to the client — the same mechanism `createResource` uses for any async resource, query-related or not.\n4. On the client, once the snapshot arrives, Solid Query hydrates the query cache from it internally, before your component ever sees a loading state for that query.\n\nSo the three steps you'd normally implement by hand — prefetch, dehydrate, hydrate — collapse into \"call `useQuery` (or prefetch with `queryClient.query`), and let Solid's streaming take care of the rest.\" The tradeoff is that Solid Query decides what gets serialized and when, rather than you calling `dehydrate` with your own filtering logic.\n\nOne consequence worth calling out: a query that resolved on the server, and is read with a `staleTime` (or without `initialData`), does not refetch on mount when it hydrates on the client, since Solid Query already knows it has fresh-enough data from the server render. It behaves the same as any other successful query after that: `staleTime` governs whether it refetches on the next trigger (window focus, a new mount, `invalidateQueries`, and so on).\n\n## Initial setup\n\nAs with any server-rendered app, create the `QueryClient` per request rather than at module scope, so cached data from one visitor's request is never shared with another's:\n\n```tsx\n// src/app.tsx\nimport { MetaProvider, Title } from '@solidjs/meta'\nimport { Router } from '@solidjs/router'\nimport { FileRoutes } from '@solidjs/start/router'\nimport { Suspense } from 'solid-js'\nimport { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  // Constructing the QueryClient inside the component (rather than at module\n  // scope) means SolidStart creates a fresh one for every request, so data\n  // is never shared between users.\n  const queryClient = new QueryClient({\n    defaultOptions: {\n      queries: {\n        // staleTime governs refetches from later triggers (a new mount, window\n        // focus, and so on) — see Solid Query's hydration model above for why\n        // the very first mount after SSR doesn't need it to skip a refetch.\n        staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n      },\n    },\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n      <Router\n        root={(props) => (\n          <MetaProvider>\n            <Title>My App</Title>\n            <Suspense>{props.children}</Suspense>\n          </MetaProvider>\n        )}\n      >\n        <FileRoutes />\n      </Router>\n    </QueryClientProvider>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe `<Suspense>` boundary matters: `useQuery` suspends while a query is pending, both on the server (so SolidStart can wait for it, or stream past it) and on the client (during the very first render, before hydration data arrives).\n\n## Using `useQuery` directly\n\nBecause hydration is automatic, the simplest way to get server-rendered data is to just call `useQuery` where you need it — no separate prefetch step required:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\nimport { For, Suspense } from 'solid-js'\n\nfunction Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryFn: fetchPosts,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n      <ul>\n        <For each={query.data}>{(post) => <li>{post.title}</li>}</For>\n      </ul>\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nOn the server, this fetches `posts`, waits for it (because of the surrounding `<Suspense>`), and streams the resolved value down. On the client, that value hydrates the cache before `Posts` ever renders a loading state — as long as the client also renders inside a `<Suspense>` boundary, which the `<Suspense>` in `app.tsx` already provides.\n\nWrap the same query in Solid's `ErrorBoundary` to handle a failure. On the server, a failed query always throws to the nearest `ErrorBoundary` — see [Error handling](#error-handling) below for why, and for what it takes to get the same behavior on the client too:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\nimport { ErrorBoundary, For, Suspense } from 'solid-js'\n\nfunction Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryFn: fetchPosts,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <ErrorBoundary\n      fallback={(err, reset) => (\n        <div>\n          <div>Error: {err.message}</div>\n          <button\n            onClick={async () => {\n              await query.refetch()\n              reset()\n            }}\n          >\n            Retry\n          </button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    >\n      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n        <ul>\n          <For each={query.data}>{(post) => <li>{post.title}</li>}</For>\n        </ul>\n      </Suspense>\n    </ErrorBoundary>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Prefetching with `route.load`\n\nTo prefetch a query from a SolidStart route's `load` function, call `queryClient.query(...)`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { noop, useQueryClient } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\n\nexport const route = {\n  load: () => {\n    const queryClient = useQueryClient()\n    queryClient.query(postsQueryOptions()).catch(noop)\n  },\n}\n\nexport default function Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => postsQueryOptions())\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n`.catch(noop)` keeps a failed prefetch from aborting routing — whatever reads the query afterwards (`useQuery`) will surface the failure on its own. See [Error handling](#error-handling) below for how that failure surfaces differently on the server and the client.\n\nSharing the query options object (`postsQueryOptions()` above) between `route.load` and the component that reads the data, rather than repeating the `queryKey`/`queryFn` in both places, keeps them from drifting apart — see [`queryOptions`](./query-options.md).\n\n## Streaming with `deferStream`\n\nBy default, a query streams to the client as soon as it resolves on the server. Set `deferStream: true` to hold the server's response until that query resolves instead — useful for data that has to be present in the very first response, such as SEO meta tags:\n\n```tsx\nconst query = useQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['user'],\n  queryFn: fetchUser,\n  deferStream: true,\n}))\n```\n\n`deferStream` only affects when the _server_ flushes that query's chunk to the client. Setting it on every query on a page makes the server wait for all of them before sending anything.\n\n## Error handling\n\nOn the server, `useQuery` always throws when a query fails — regardless of the `throwOnError` option — so it's caught by the nearest `<ErrorBoundary>` rather than surfacing as `query.isError`. This is different from the client, where a failed query reports `isError`/`error` unless you opt in to throwing with `throwOnError: true`. Retries are also disabled on the server, so a query throws on its first failure rather than retrying first.\n\nIf a query is read with `useQuery` on both the server and the client, set `throwOnError: true` on it explicitly so the client matches what the server already does — otherwise the same failure throws during SSR but only sets `isError` once it's running on the client:\n\n```tsx\nconst query = useQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['user'],\n  queryFn: fetchUser,\n  throwOnError: true,\n}))\n```\n\nA `route.load` prefetch isn't affected by this server-only override — `queryClient.query(...)` doesn't take a `throwOnError` option at all, so it simply rejects when the query fails, the same on the server and the client. Catch it (or let it propagate) however fits the route; see [Prefetching with `route.load`](#prefetching-with-routeload) above for the `.catch(noop)` pattern used when a failed prefetch shouldn't abort routing.\n\n## Tips, Tricks and Caveats\n\n### Staleness is measured from when the query was fetched on the server\n\nA query's staleness is based on `dataUpdatedAt`, which is set to when it resolved on the server, not when the client hydrates it. Because `staleTime` defaults to `0`, an SSR'd query without an explicit `staleTime` is treated as stale as soon as its next trigger (window focus, a new mount past the first one, `invalidateQueries`, and so on) checks it, and may refetch in the background then — the first mount on the client is still exempt, per [Solid Query's hydration model](#solid-querys-hydration-model) above. Setting a `staleTime` above `0` (as in the Initial setup example above) avoids this if you don't want it.\n\n### High memory consumption on server\n\nBecause the `QueryClient` is created fresh for every request, its cache is held in memory for the duration of that request. Under high request volume this can add up. `gcTime` defaults to `Infinity` on the server, so unless you configure it lower, memory for a request's cache isn't reclaimed automatically — in most cases this is fine, since the whole `QueryClient` instance becomes eligible for garbage collection once the request finishes and nothing references it anymore.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: ssr\ntitle: SSR\n---\n\nThis guide covers using Solid Query with server rendering, specifically with [SolidStart](https://start.solidjs.com/).\n\nIf you haven't already, it's worth reading the [Prefetching & Router Integration](./prefetching.md) guide first.\n\n## Solid Query's hydration model\n\nSolid Query's `useQuery` is built on top of Solid's own [`createResource`](https://docs.solidjs.com/reference/basic-reactivity/create-resource), rather than a `dehydrate`/`hydrate` pair you call yourself. That has a consequence for this guide: **there is no `dehydrate`, `hydrate`, or `HydrationBoundary` in your application code.**\n\nInstead, when `useQuery` runs on the server:\n\n1. It resolves the query through the same `QueryClient` cache a client render would use, with `retry` forced off and `throwOnError` forced on — see [Error handling](#error-handling) below.\n2. The resolved value is a plain, serializable snapshot of the query result (functions like `refetch` are stripped, since they can't cross the network).\n3. Solid's own resource streaming carries that snapshot down to the client — the same mechanism `createResource` uses for any async resource, query-related or not.\n4. On the client, once the snapshot arrives, Solid Query hydrates the query cache from it internally, before your component ever sees a loading state for that query.\n\nSo the three steps you'd normally implement by hand — prefetch, dehydrate, hydrate — collapse into \"call `useQuery` (or prefetch with `queryClient.query`), and let Solid's streaming take care of the rest.\" The tradeoff is that Solid Query decides what gets serialized and when, rather than you calling `dehydrate` with your own filtering logic.\n\nOne consequence worth calling out: a query that resolved on the server, and is read with a `staleTime` (or without `initialData`), does not refetch on mount when it hydrates on the client, since Solid Query already knows it has fresh-enough data from the server render. It behaves the same as any other successful query after that: `staleTime` governs whether it refetches on the next trigger (window focus, a new mount, `invalidateQueries`, and so on).\n\n## Initial setup\n\nAs with any server-rendered app, create the `QueryClient` per request rather than at module scope, so cached data from one visitor's request is never shared with another's:\n\n```tsx\n// src/app.tsx\nimport { MetaProvider, Title } from '@solidjs/meta'\nimport { Router } from '@solidjs/router'\nimport { FileRoutes } from '@solidjs/start/router'\nimport { Suspense } from 'solid-js'\nimport { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  // Constructing the QueryClient inside the component (rather than at module\n  // scope) means SolidStart creates a fresh one for every request, so data\n  // is never shared between users.\n  const queryClient = new QueryClient({\n    defaultOptions: {\n      queries: {\n        // staleTime governs refetches from later triggers (a new mount, window\n        // focus, and so on) — see Solid Query's hydration model above for why\n        // the very first mount after SSR doesn't need it to skip a refetch.\n        staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n      },\n    },\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n      <Router\n        root={(props) => (\n          <MetaProvider>\n            <Title>My App</Title>\n            <Suspense>{props.children}</Suspense>\n          </MetaProvider>\n        )}\n      >\n        <FileRoutes />\n      </Router>\n    </QueryClientProvider>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe `<Suspense>` boundary matters: `useQuery` suspends while a query is pending, both on the server (so SolidStart can wait for it, or stream past it) and on the client (during the very first render, before hydration data arrives).\n\n## Using `useQuery` directly\n\nBecause hydration is automatic, the simplest way to get server-rendered data is to just call `useQuery` where you need it — no separate prefetch step required:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\nimport { For, Suspense } from 'solid-js'\n\nfunction Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryFn: fetchPosts,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n      <ul>\n        <For each={query.data}>{(post) => <li>{post.title}</li>}</For>\n      </ul>\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nOn the server, this fetches `posts`, waits for it (because of the surrounding `<Suspense>`), and streams the resolved value down. On the client, that value hydrates the cache before `Posts` ever renders a loading state — as long as the client also renders inside a `<Suspense>` boundary, which the `<Suspense>` in `app.tsx` already provides.\n\nWrap the same query in Solid's `ErrorBoundary` to handle a failure. On the server, a failed query always throws to the nearest `ErrorBoundary` — see [Error handling](#error-handling) below for why, and for what it takes to get the same behavior on the client too:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\nimport { ErrorBoundary, For, Suspense } from 'solid-js'\n\nfunction Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\n    queryFn: fetchPosts,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <ErrorBoundary\n      fallback={(err, reset) => (\n        <div>\n          <div>Error: {err.message}</div>\n          <button\n            onClick={async () => {\n              await query.refetch()\n              reset()\n            }}\n          >\n            Retry\n          </button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    >\n      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n        <ul>\n          <For each={query.data}>{(post) => <li>{post.title}</li>}</For>\n        </ul>\n      </Suspense>\n    </ErrorBoundary>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Prefetching with `route.load`\n\nTo prefetch a query from a SolidStart route's `load` function, call `queryClient.query(...)`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { noop, useQueryClient } from '@tanstack/solid-query'\n\nexport const route = {\n  load: () => {\n    const queryClient = useQueryClient()\n    queryClient.query(postsQueryOptions()).catch(noop)\n  },\n}\n\nexport default function Posts() {\n  const query = useQuery(() => postsQueryOptions())\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n`.catch(noop)` keeps a failed prefetch from aborting routing — whatever reads the query afterwards (`useQuery`) will surface the failure on its own. See [Error handling](#error-handling) below for how that failure surfaces differently on the server and the client.\n\nSharing the query options object (`postsQueryOptions()` above) between `route.load` and the component that reads the data, rather than repeating the `queryKey`/`queryFn` in both places, keeps them from drifting apart — see [`queryOptions`](./query-options.md).\n\n## Streaming with `deferStream`\n\nBy default, a query streams to the client as soon as it resolves on the server. Set `deferStream: true` to hold the server's response until that query resolves instead — useful for data that has to be present in the very first response, such as SEO meta tags:\n\n```tsx\nconst query = useQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['user'],\n  queryFn: fetchUser,\n  deferStream: true,\n}))\n```\n\n`deferStream` only affects when the _server_ flushes that query's chunk to the client. Setting it on every query on a page makes the server wait for all of them before sending anything.\n\n## Error handling\n\nOn the server, `useQuery` always throws when a query fails — regardless of the `throwOnError` option — so it's caught by the nearest `<ErrorBoundary>` rather than surfacing as `query.isError`. This is different from the client, where a failed query reports `isError`/`error` unless you opt in to throwing with `throwOnError: true`. Retries are also disabled on the server, so a query throws on its first failure rather than retrying first.\n\nIf a query is read with `useQuery` on both the server and the client, set `throwOnError: true` on it explicitly so the client matches what the server already does — otherwise the same failure throws during SSR but only sets `isError` once it's running on the client:\n\n```tsx\nconst query = useQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['user'],\n  queryFn: fetchUser,\n  throwOnError: true,\n}))\n```\n\nA `route.load` prefetch isn't affected by this server-only override — `queryClient.query(...)` doesn't take a `throwOnError` option at all, so it simply rejects when the query fails, the same on the server and the client. Catch it (or let it propagate) however fits the route; see [Prefetching with `route.load`](#prefetching-with-routeload) above for the `.catch(noop)` pattern used when a failed prefetch shouldn't abort routing.\n\n## Tips, Tricks and Caveats\n\n### Staleness is measured from when the query was fetched on the server\n\nA query's staleness is based on `dataUpdatedAt`, which is set to when it resolved on the server, not when the client hydrates it. Because `staleTime` defaults to `0`, an SSR'd query without an explicit `staleTime` is treated as stale as soon as its next trigger (window focus, a new mount past the first one, `invalidateQueries`, and so on) checks it, and may refetch in the background then — the first mount on the client is still exempt, per [Solid Query's hydration model](#solid-querys-hydration-model) above. Setting a `staleTime` above `0` (as in the Initial setup example above) avoids this if you don't want it.\n\n### High memory consumption on server\n\nBecause the `QueryClient` is created fresh for every request, its cache is held in memory for the duration of that request. Under high request volume this can add up. `gcTime` defaults to `Infinity` on the server, so unless you configure it lower, memory for a request's cache isn't reclaimed automatically — in most cases this is fine, since the whole `QueryClient` instance becomes eligible for garbage collection once the request finishes and nothing references it anymore.\n"}
